# Ghola: Tiered Memory for Teams of Agents (v1a)

A shared-brain memory system for AI agents, organized into three tiers
by role (working, episodic, semantic) rather than by storage. Working
memory is per-session and local; episodic is raw, per-user, and
centralized; semantic is distilled, shared, and governed by cognitive
primitives (ACT-R activation, Hebbian learning, Bayesian confidence,
contradiction detection).

Agents speak to a local Go service. The local service owns working
memory directly and calls Chapterhouse for everything above that tier.
Two consolidation pipelines move information upward: Pipeline A is
continuous and lossless (working -> episodic, no LLM); Pipeline B is
nightly and lossy (episodic -> semantic, LLM-assisted). The local
service never holds Postgres credentials -- only a per-user Chapterhouse
API key.

v1a ships the minimum viable cut: working + episodic + semantic, both
pipelines, cognitive primitives, a shared team brain. Five other memory
types (procedural, prospective, spatial, flashbulb, priming) and
LongMemEval benchmarking are explicitly deferred.

---

CONSTRAINT: three_tier_decomposition
  Memory is decomposed into exactly three tiers, each with a single role:
    - WORKING: per-session ephemeral tree of turns. Local to user device.
    - EPISODIC: raw preservation of all sessions, per-user partitioned,
      centralized. Verbatim content; no summarization.
    - SEMANTIC: distilled facts, shared across team, centralized.
      Cognitive primitives apply at this tier only.
  Adding a fourth tier, collapsing two tiers, or summarizing at the
  episodic tier is out of scope for v1a.

CONSTRAINT: two_substrates
  Working lives in SQLite with sqlite-vec and FTS5. Episodic and
  semantic live in Postgres under separate schemas (episodic, semantic)
  of one database. No other substrates (no ChromaDB, no dedicated
  graph store, no Redis, no Elasticsearch) are introduced in v1a.

CONSTRAINT: one_canonical_interface
  Agents see a single set of 11 operations regardless of transport.
  The Ghola local service exposes them twice -- over MCP (stdio or
  localhost HTTP) for MCP-aware agents (Claude Code) and over HTTP/JSON
  for non-MCP agents (pi-mono). Both surfaces delegate to the same
  core library in the same process. No business logic lives in the
  protocol wrappers.

CONSTRAINT: local_first_working_centralized_rest
  Working writes never block on the network. The local service writes
  to an embedded SQLite file on the user device. Episodic and semantic
  live on team infrastructure. Agent devices hold only a Chapterhouse
  API key; Postgres credentials never leave team infrastructure.
  Private-by-default access is enforced by ACL tables (episodic.shares),
  not physical isolation.

CONSTRAINT: raw_episodic
  Episodic is stored verbatim, not summarized. Every turn promoted from
  working lands as its own row in episodic.turns with vector, FTS, and
  entity indexes. Compression into cohesive summaries happens at the
  semantic tier (Pipeline B) or not at all. This matches MemPalace's
  retrieval pattern and keeps Pipeline A cheap and lossless.

CONSTRAINT: two_pipelines
  Exactly two consolidation pipelines exist:
    - PIPELINE_A: working -> episodic. Inside the Ghola local service.
      Continuous (timer + size + explicit triggers). No LLM. Watermark-
      driven and idempotent on replay.
    - PIPELINE_B: episodic -> semantic. Inside Chapterhouse. Nightly
      cron at 02:00 local. Cross-user (ACL-respecting). LLM-assisted.
      Entity co-occurrence >= 3 distinct sessions is the inclusion
      threshold.

CONSTRAINT: cognitive_primitives_semantic_only
  ACT-R activation, Ebbinghaus decay, Hebbian weight updates, Bayesian
  confidence updates, contradiction detection, softplus compositing,
  and archival run only over semantic.mnemes and semantic.associations.
  Episodic and working are inert with respect to these primitives.

CONSTRAINT: pg_ghola_v2_scope
  pg_ghola v2 manages exactly five tables under the semantic schema:
  mnemes, associations, co_activation_queue, contradiction_queue,
  contradiction_candidates. The v1 sub_mnemes, clusters, mneme_clusters,
  thalamic_gating, temporal_tokens, and related tables are removed.
  Extension version advances to 0.2.0. Migrations from v1 are not
  supported; v1a is a drop-and-recreate on production.

CONSTRAINT: dune_naming
  Components carry names that describe role, not substrate, so
  substrates can change without renaming:
    - SIETCH = working-memory store (currently SQLite)
    - CHAPTERHOUSE = shared team service (Go + Postgres)
    - MELANGE = embedding service (swappable model behind HTTP)
    - MENTAT = distillation LLM (swappable model behind HTTP)
  No agent-facing API surfaces the substrate name.

CONSTRAINT: swappable_models
  Melange and Mentat are HTTP services with deployment-time model
  choices. Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B is the v1a embedding default but BGE,
  Nomic, or any sentence-transformer producing 1024d vectors is
  acceptable. Gemma is the v1a Mentat default on local vLLM; any
  capable local LLM is acceptable. No system behavior is coupled to
  a specific model.

CONSTRAINT: dev_prod_parity
  docker-compose up on a developer laptop produces the same binaries
  and same code paths as production, differing only by environment
  variables (CHAPTERHOUSE_URL, MELANGE_URL, MENTAT_URL). The local
  service, Chapterhouse, Postgres, Melange, and Mentat all run on
  localhost in development and on team infrastructure (K3s cluster,
  NUC) in production.

CONSTRAINT: v1a_deferrals
  The following are explicitly not part of v1a and must not creep into
  implementation:
    - Procedural, prospective, spatial, flashbulb memory tiers
    - Priming (recency-weighted semantic promotion into working context)
    - Cross-device working-DB sync
    - LongMemEval benchmarking of the new architecture
    - Encoding-strategy iteration (late chunking variants, multi-scale)
    - Multi-tenant Chapterhouse hosting independent teams
    - Federated cross-team semantic sharing
    - LLM-based entity extraction upgrades (regex plus lightweight NER
      is sufficient)

---

DATA: turn
  id:              identifier unique within its tier
  parent_id:       identifier of predecessor turn or null at branch root
  session_id:      owning session
  user_id:         uuid of owner
  role:            one of 'user', 'assistant', 'system', 'tool'
  content:         verbatim text body
  tool_name:       null unless role = 'tool'
  tool_input:      structured input if role = 'tool' else null
  tool_output:     structured output if role = 'tool' else null
  bookmark_label:  text label if agent explicitly marked this turn
  embedding:       1024-dimensional float vector produced by Melange
  entities:        array of extracted entity strings
  tags:            array of user-supplied string tags
  created_at:      timestamp at turn creation

DATA: mneme
  id:                    uuid
  workspace_id:          uuid of owning team
  concept:               short topic label produced by Mentat
  content:               distilled fact body produced by Mentat
  embedding:             1024-dimensional float vector over concept+content
  confidence:            float in (0, 1), Bayesian-updated
  access_count:          integer, how many recalls have touched this mneme
  last_access:           timestamp of most recent recall
  created_at:            timestamp at distillation
  state:                 'active' or 'archived'
  memory_type:           'factual', 'experiential', or 'procedural'
  tags:                  array of string tags
  entities:              array of entity strings mentioned
  source_episodic_ids:   array of episodic turn uuids that contributed
  contributor_user_ids:  array of user uuids that contributed

DATA: recall_result
  mneme_id:       uuid
  tier:           one of 'working', 'episodic', 'semantic'
  score:          final composite score, float
  content_match:  vector + FTS fusion component, float
  activation:     ACT-R base-level activation, float (semantic only)
  hebbian_boost:  association weight sum (semantic only)
  confidence:     Bayesian confidence (semantic only)
  concept:        short label, may be null for working/episodic
  content:        verbatim or distilled text

DATA: association
  src_id:           uuid of source mneme
  dst_id:           uuid of destination mneme
  association_type: 'hebbian', 'contradicts', 'supersedes', or 'supports'
  weight:           float, multiplicative-updated
  co_activations:   integer count of co-activation events
  updated_at:       timestamp

DATA: share
  owner_user_id:   uuid of sharer
  target:          'team' or 'user'
  target_id:       uuid of specific user when target = 'user', else null
  scope_type:      'session', 'branch', or 'turn'
  scope_id:        uuid of shared unit in episodic
  created_at:      timestamp

---

FUNCTION: session_start(user_id) -> session_id

  Provision a fresh working memory for this session. No network calls
  required.

  RULES:
    - Creates a new SIETCH (SQLite file) owned by user_id
    - Inserts a sessions row with started_at = now
    - Returns the newly created session_id
    - Idempotent by (user_id, client-supplied nonce) if provided

  DONE_WHEN:
    - Session exists in both the sietch file and episodic.sessions
      registry after first record of the session is promoted

  EXAMPLES:
    session_start(user_id = "alice")
      -> session_id of a new SIETCH file, zero turns
    session_start twice in quick succession with different nonces
      -> two distinct session_ids, two distinct SIETCH files

  ERRORS:
    - user_id is null -> reject with descriptive message
    - SIETCH file creation fails on disk -> surface OS error, no partial state

FUNCTION: record(session_id, parent_turn_id, role, content, tool_*) -> turn

  Append a turn to the current session's working tree. Writes hit only
  SIETCH on the hot path. Embedding is computed inline via Melange.

  RULES:
    - Embeds content via Melange and stores 1024d vector in SIETCH
    - Inserts row into SIETCH.turns with parent_turn_id linking to
      existing turn in the same session or null at branch root
    - Updates SIETCH.turns_fts for keyword search
    - Does not call Chapterhouse (Pipeline A handles promotion)
    - Returns the new turn id

  DONE_WHEN:
    - Turn is visible to recall() in the working tier within 10ms
    - Embedding is persisted before the function returns

  EXAMPLES:
    record(session=S1, parent=null, role="user", content="hi")
      -> turn T1 at branch root, embedding present, recall for "hi"
         returns T1 from working tier
    record(session=S1, parent=T1, role="assistant", content="hello")
      -> turn T2 child of T1

  ERRORS:
    - Melange unreachable -> reject with clear error; no partial turn written
    - parent_turn_id refers to a different session -> reject

FUNCTION: branch(session_id, from_turn_id) -> turn

  Create a new branch node from an existing turn, without content.
  Useful for exploratory lines of reasoning.

  RULES:
    - Inserts a SIETCH.turns row with parent_id = from_turn_id and empty content
    - Moves the session's current-pointer to the new turn
    - No embedding required for empty branches

  EXAMPLES:
    branch(session=S1, from_turn=T3)
      -> new turn T4 with parent=T3, content="", current-pointer = T4

FUNCTION: bookmark(turn_id, label) -> ok

  Attach a human-readable label to a turn. Used for explicit "remember
  this point" semantics and hybrid-C+D consolidation triggers.

  RULES:
    - Sets bookmark_label on the target turn in SIETCH
    - If promoted already, also sets bookmark_label on the episodic row

  EXAMPLES:
    bookmark(turn=T5, label="shipped migration 0042")
      -> T5.bookmark_label stored; subsequent Pipeline A pass propagates
         it to episodic.turns.bookmark_label

FUNCTION: navigate(session_id, turn_id) -> ok

  Move the session's current-pointer to an existing turn in the same
  session's tree.

  EXAMPLES:
    navigate(session=S1, turn=T2)
      -> current pointer moves to T2 without creating a new turn

FUNCTION: recall(session_id, query_text, query_embedding, limit, filters) -> ordered recall_results

  Return merged, tier-attributed results across working + episodic +
  semantic.

  RULES:
    - Fans out to three sources in parallel:
        working  (SIETCH: vec0 nearest-neighbour + FTS5 over turns)
        episodic (Chapterhouse: /v1/episodic/query)
        semantic (Chapterhouse: /v1/semantic/query)
    - Merges results by final score, capped at limit
    - Each result carries tier in {'working','episodic','semantic'}
    - Filters may scope to session_id, entities, tags, role, or
      date range; unsupported filters in a given tier are ignored for
      that tier (never fail the whole recall)
    - If Chapterhouse is unreachable, working-only results are returned
      with a partial flag; the call still succeeds

  DONE_WHEN:
    - A query whose answer lives in working returns within 50ms P50
    - A query whose answer lives in semantic returns within 300ms P50
    - Tier attribution is correct: working-sourced rows are tagged
      'working', etc.
    - Results are sorted by score descending

  EXAMPLES:
    recall(session=S1, query="when did we migrate Postgres?",
           limit=10, filters={})
      -> up to 10 rows; the pre-migration turn in working appears first
         if recent enough, followed by episodic context and semantic
         fact "Team migrated to Postgres 16 on 2026-03-01"

    recall with Chapterhouse offline
      -> returns only working-tier rows with partial=true

  ERRORS:
    - query_embedding has wrong dimensionality -> reject
    - session_id does not exist -> reject

FUNCTION: forget(memory_id, tier) -> ok

  Mark a memory for deletion in the specified tier.

  RULES:
    - working: remove the SIETCH row outright
    - episodic: soft-delete (content -> '[forgotten]', embedding -> null)
      to preserve tree structure for causal reasoning
    - semantic: set state = 'archived' on the mneme; never hard-delete
      from semantic in v1a

  EXAMPLES:
    forget(memory=T7, tier='working')
      -> T7 gone from SIETCH
    forget(memory=E42, tier='episodic')
      -> E42's content becomes '[forgotten]', embedding nulled, parent
         and child pointers preserved
    forget(memory=M9, tier='semantic')
      -> M9.state = 'archived', no longer returned by recall

FUNCTION: share(scope_type, scope_id, target) -> share

  Grant visibility of a session, branch, or turn to the team or a
  specific user.

  RULES:
    - Calls Chapterhouse /v1/episodic/share
    - target = 'team' shares with the entire workspace
    - target = 'user' requires target_id; shares only with that user
    - scope_type in {'session','branch','turn'} and scope_id is the
      corresponding episodic uuid
    - Creates exactly one episodic.shares row; idempotent on
      (owner, scope_id, target, target_id)

  EXAMPLES:
    share(scope='session', scope_id=E-S1, target='team')
      -> other users' recall queries now include S1's turns from
         episodic with tier='episodic' and attribution preserved

FUNCTION: consolidate(session_id) -> ok

  Force Pipeline A to run for the current session right now, rather
  than waiting for the next timer tick.

  RULES:
    - Blocks until the flush completes or reports failure
    - Respects watermark idempotence (same turns never ingested twice)

  EXAMPLES:
    consolidate(session=S1)
      -> episodic.turns grows by the count of unpromoted turns;
         sietch.pipeline_a_state.last_consolidated_turn_id advances

FUNCTION: session_end(session_id) -> ok

  Mark session closed and run a final Pipeline A flush plus per-branch
  coherence pass.

  RULES:
    - Final Pipeline A run for remaining unpromoted turns
    - For each terminal branch, trigger a coherence pass
    - Marks episodic.sessions.ended_at
    - SIETCH file is retained for 24h after this call, then garbage
      collected; recall continues to see working-tier rows from it
      until GC

  EXAMPLES:
    session_end(session=S1) immediately after last turn
      -> episodic grows to match sietch count; sietch still queryable
         until GC at session_end + 24h

FUNCTION: list_sessions(user_id, limit, cursor) -> sessions[]

  Enumerate the user's episodic sessions, newest first.

  RULES:
    - Paginated via opaque cursor
    - Returns session id, started_at, ended_at, turn_count, summary
    - Respects ACL: includes sessions shared to user_id as well

FUNCTION: feedback(mneme_id, signal) -> mneme

  Adjust confidence on a semantic mneme based on agent-reported outcome.

  RULES:
    - signal in {'confirm','refute'} maps to evidence in
      {0.9, 0.1} for Bayesian update
    - Calls Chapterhouse /v1/semantic/feedback which runs
      bayesian_update(prior=current_confidence, evidence=signal_value)
    - Returns the updated mneme with new confidence

  EXAMPLES:
    feedback(mneme=M9, signal='confirm') with prior 0.5
      -> new confidence ~ 0.9 (clamped to [0.025, 0.975])
    feedback(mneme=M9, signal='refute') with prior 0.9
      -> new confidence ~ 0.5

---

FUNCTION: pipeline_a_tick(session_id) -> promoted_count

  One tick of the working -> episodic promoter. Runs inside the Ghola
  local service.

  RULES:
    - Reads turns with id > sietch.pipeline_a_state.last_consolidated_turn_id
      for the given session
    - Extracts entities via regex + lightweight NER (no LLM)
    - Copies turn rows and entity arrays to Chapterhouse via
      POST /v1/episodic/ingest
    - On HTTP 2xx from Chapterhouse, advances
      last_consolidated_turn_id to max(read turn ids)
    - On failure, does not advance the watermark; next tick retries
      the same turns
    - Never mutates turn content during promotion (lossless)
    - Triggered by one of: 5-minute timer, 10 new turns since last run,
      size threshold (>5000 unpromoted turns or >50 MiB SIETCH),
      explicit consolidate() call, or session_end()

  DONE_WHEN:
    - Replaying the same tick after a retry never double-inserts rows
      (UPSERT on (id) in episodic)
    - Watermark progresses monotonically
    - A 1-hour synthetic load at 1 turn/sec drains without stalls

  EXAMPLES:
    Ticking a session with 20 new turns since last watermark
      -> Chapterhouse ingest accepts 20 turns, watermark jumps 20,
         episodic.turns grows by 20

    Ticking a session with no new turns
      -> no HTTP call made, watermark unchanged

    Ticking when Chapterhouse returns 500
      -> watermark unchanged, log line emitted, next tick retries

  ERRORS:
    - Embedding already persisted but Chapterhouse 500s
      -> retry on next tick; never log embedding contents
    - Unexpected 4xx from Chapterhouse -> fail loudly; require operator

FUNCTION: pipeline_a_coherence_pass(branch_root_id) -> episodic_summary

  Hybrid-C+D coherence pass: when a branch goes terminal (no new
  descendants for >N minutes) or on session_end(), rewrite its
  fine-grained deltas into a cohesive record with branch metadata.

  RULES:
    - Does not remove the per-turn rows; adds a branch_summary row
      (or updates an existing one) pointing at the constituent turn uuids
    - In v1a, "rewrite" is concatenation plus structured branch metadata
      (entity union, tag union, first/last timestamp, role distribution)
    - No LLM call in v1a (deferred to later iterations)

FUNCTION: pipeline_b_run(window_start, window_end) -> distilled_count

  Nightly pattern detector + Mentat distillation. Runs inside
  Chapterhouse.

  RULES:
    - Scans episodic.turns with ingested_at in [window_start, window_end]
      across all users subject to ACL (owner or explicit share)
    - Groups by entity pair co-occurrence; admits a pair only when
      co-occurring in >= 3 distinct sessions
    - For each admitted pair, assembles a batch of representative
      turns and calls Mentat
    - Mentat returns JSON {concept, content, memory_type, entities};
      malformed responses are rejected and logged
    - For each valid distilled mneme:
        a) embed via Melange
        b) search semantic.mnemes for nearest existing mneme
        c) if HNSW cosine > 0.9 -> strengthen existing via Bayesian
           update on confidence; append contributor_user_ids and
           source_episodic_ids
        d) else insert new row, enqueue co-activation and
           contradiction jobs
    - pg_ghola v2 workers (Hebbian, contradiction, consolidation)
      drain their queues independently after pipeline_b_run returns

  DONE_WHEN:
    - Running on a 24h window with three sessions mentioning
      (Postgres, CNPG) produces exactly one new or strengthened mneme
      about that pair
    - A synthetic contradictory mneme pair appears in
      semantic.contradiction_candidates after workers drain
    - Idempotent on re-run: same window produces the same semantic
      outcome (strengthening, not duplication)

  EXAMPLES:
    pipeline_b_run(window = 2026-04-19 00:00:00 .. 2026-04-20 00:00:00)
      on a DB seeded with three Postgres-migration sessions
      -> one new mneme with concept "Postgres migration" or similar,
         contributor_user_ids covering all three authors,
         source_episodic_ids covering representative turns

    pipeline_b_run on a window with no recurring entity pairs
      -> zero new mnemes, no Mentat calls

    pipeline_b_run where Mentat returns invalid JSON
      -> offending batch logged with prompt hash, skipped;
         other batches proceed

  ERRORS:
    - Mentat unreachable -> skip pipeline_b_run; retry next night
    - Postgres transaction failure during dedup -> rollback entire
      distilled batch; watermark unchanged

---

CONSTRAINT: local_service_loopback_only
  The Ghola local service binds to 127.0.0.1:7421 only and rejects
  non-loopback connections at the middleware layer. This is enforced
  whether running as `ghola` (HTTP) or `ghola-mcp` (MCP-over-HTTP).

CONSTRAINT: api_key_auth
  All Chapterhouse calls from the local service carry an
  Authorization: Bearer <api-key> header. Keys are per-user, rotated
  on request, and hashed-at-rest (never stored plaintext). Requests
  without a valid key are rejected 401 before any handler runs.

CONSTRAINT: upsert_on_ingest
  episodic.turns INSERT operations must upsert on (id) so that
  pipeline_a_tick retries are safe. Conflicts do not overwrite
  embedding, content, or timestamps; they are no-ops.

CONSTRAINT: no_postgres_credentials_on_devices
  No user device ever holds Postgres credentials. Only Chapterhouse
  holds pgx connection strings. The deploy script on user machines
  requires only a CHAPTERHOUSE_URL and CHAPTERHOUSE_API_KEY.

CONSTRAINT: composable_dev_stack
  `docker compose up` from /home/loganb/ghola/deploy/docker-compose
  must produce a working end-to-end stack (Postgres with pg_ghola v2
  extension, Chapterhouse, Melange, optional Mentat, local Ghola
  service) ready to serve record/recall in under 30 seconds on a
  laptop with image layers pre-pulled.

---

FUNCTION: v1a_integration_acceptance() -> releasable v1a

  End-to-end acceptance gate. v1a is shippable when all sub-criteria
  hold simultaneously on a freshly provisioned stack.

  DONE_WHEN:
    - A pi-mono agent and a Claude Code MCP agent both record and
      recall against the same local service and see each other's turns
    - Pipeline A runs continuously without error over a 1-hour synthetic
      session at 1 turn/sec; watermark advances monotonically;
      episodic grows by the expected count
    - Pipeline B runs on the nightly cron; synthetic repetition of
      (entity_a, entity_b) in >=3 sessions produces one distilled
      mneme; cognitive primitives fire (associations row present,
      contradiction candidates evaluated)
    - A session shared by User A via share() appears in User B's
      recall with tier='episodic' and attribution preserved
    - CREATE EXTENSION pg_ghola on an empty production Postgres
      succeeds with the v2 five-table schema and no v1 leftovers
    - A developer laptop with only the ghola binary + a
      CHAPTERHOUSE_API_KEY env var can run the pi-mono sample app;
      no Postgres credentials reachable from the device
    - `docker compose up` in deploy/docker-compose reaches healthy
      state in under 30 seconds on a machine with cached images

  EXAMPLES:
    Cold install on a new laptop + API key
      -> record/recall round-trip succeeds; semantic recall returns
         team knowledge; no Postgres envvars present

    Two agents on the same laptop with different transports
      -> both see each other's turns in working and in episodic
         after Pipeline A tick

  ERRORS:
    - Any sub-criterion fails -> v1a is not releasable; open a
      diagnostic iteration rather than shipping partially

